What you should be able to explain afterwards
By the end of the activity you should be able to explain:

what information a CIF contains;
the difference between the asymmetric unit and the complete unit cell;
how symmetry operations generate equivalent atomic positions;
what a Wyckoff position and its multiplicity represent;
why a numerical symmetry search requires a tolerance;
why visualizing a structure is useful for inspection but does not replace a crystallographic analysis.

Your answers are written directly in the notebooks. No separate report is required.

What you will produce
At the end of the activity your notebook should contain:

the optimized MgO lattice parameter and cell volume;
a comparison between the experimental starting geometry and the static DFT equilibrium geometry;
an explanation of why the Mg and O fractional coordinates do not move;
an energy–volume dataset around the optimized structure;
a third-order Birch–Murnaghan (BM3) fit;
the fitted \(V_0\), \(K_0\) and \(K'_0\);
an \(E(V)\) plot;
a \(P(V)\) plot reconstructed from the fitted energy relation;
a short interpretation of the bulk modulus.

The repeated extraction and fitting steps are handled by Quantas so that the scientific work remains focused on interpretation rather than transcription.

What you will calculate
You will calculate two phonon datasets yourself:

the primitive-cell frequencies at \(\Gamma\);
a \(2\times2\times2\) SCELPHONO calculation.

Validated \(3\times3\times3\) and \(4\times4\times4\) datasets will be supplied by the instructor. They are deliberately provided because their computational cost is not the learning objective of this course.
All calculations use:

the optimized MgO geometry from Laboratory 3;
the SHRINK value that you selected in Laboratory 2;
the same DFT functional, basis set and numerical thresholds used previously.

The self-consistent field cycle
The Kohn–Sham equations depend on the electron density, but the density is also obtained from their solution. CRYSTAL therefore solves the problem self-consistently:

start from an initial electron density;
construct the Kohn–Sham matrix;
solve for the electronic states;
build a new density;
compare the new solution with the previous one;
repeat until the chosen convergence criterion is satisfied.

This is the SCF cycle.
CRYSTAL prints the total energy during the iterations. A successful calculation eventually contains a line of the form:
SCF ENDED - CONVERGENCE ON ENERGY
The analysis tools used in this laboratory deliberately require this convergence marker. The last printed energy of an interrupted or unconverged calculation is not accepted as a valid result.

Reading the result
We will inspect one output manually before using the parser.
The output reports:

the Monkhorst shrinking factors;
the number of k-points in the irreducible Brillouin zone;
the sequence of SCF cycles;
the final converged total energy;
elapsed and CPU time;
the normal termination of the calculation.

This manual inspection matters. Automated parsing should save us from repetitive transcription, not hide the origin of the data.

What is an equation of state?
In thermodynamics, an equation of state is a mathematical relation between state variables. For a solid under compression we are especially interested in the relation between pressure and volume.
In the present static calculation, the EOS is parameterized by fitting the computed \(E(V)\) data. The fit provides a smooth representation of the energy surface and therefore, through \(P=-dE/dV\), a smooth pressure–volume relation.
We will use a third-order Birch–Murnaghan equation of state (BM3).
The purpose here is not to derive the finite-strain formalism. The important point is what the fitted parameters mean:

\(E_0\): minimum static energy;
\(V_0\): equilibrium volume;
\(K_0\): bulk modulus at equilibrium;
\(K'_0\): first pressure derivative of the bulk modulus.

The fitted \(V_0\) should be close to the volume obtained from the direct geometry optimization. If it is not, that discrepancy is something to investigate.

From phonons to statistical thermodynamics
In statistical thermodynamics, each harmonic phonon behaves as a quantum harmonic oscillator. Once the frequency spectrum is known, temperature determines how strongly each oscillator is populated.
Quantas uses the q-resolved phonon spectrum to calculate quantities including:

zero-point vibrational energy;
vibrational internal energy;
vibrational Helmholtz free energy;
vibrational entropy;
isochoric heat capacity \(C_V\).

This is the computational version of the connection developed in the statistical-thermodynamics part of the course: the phonon spectrum defines the available vibrational states, while thermal populations determine macroscopic entropy and heat capacity.
The Quantas harmonic workflow is documented here:
https://quantas.readthedocs.io/en/2.0.0-beta/api/ha.html

Laboratory path
The practical work will progressively connect crystal structures, energetic models, physical properties and mineral thermodynamics:

crystal structures, CIF files and symmetry;
first-principles energy and geometry optimization;
energy-volume data and equations of state;
vibrations, phonons and thermodynamic properties.

Short scientific questions are answered directly in the laboratory notebooks. The notebooks are part of your personal workspace and should be saved and synchronized regularly.

Stop the Codespace






ImportantClosing the browser tab does not stop the Codespace



Always stop the Codespace explicitly when you finish working.


The most reliable method is:

open https://github.com/codespaces;
locate your Mineral Science Codespace;
select the … menu;
select Stop codespace.

You can also stop it from inside Visual Studio Code:

press Ctrl+Shift+P on Windows/Linux or Cmd+Shift+P on macOS;
search for Codespaces: Stop Current Codespace;
select the command.

Stopping a Codespace ends the running virtual machine while preserving saved files for the next session. It also avoids unnecessary compute usage.
Do not delete your Codespace as part of the normal workflow. Delete it only when explicitly instructed or when you are certain that all required work has already been synchronized to GitHub.

What you need on your own computer
You need:

a modern web browser;
a GitHub account;
an internet connection;
later in the course, an SSH/SFTP client for access to CluMiner.

Everything, from Python environment, Jupyter notebooks, and other tools are provided inside the Codespace.
Continue with Create your personal workspace.

What you will produce
By the end of the activity you should have:

CRYSTAL outputs for SHRINK values 2–9;
a table containing the reciprocal-space sampling, total energy and timings;
energy-difference values in both hartree and kJ mol\(^{-1}\);
convergence and computational-cost plots;
a justified choice of the SHRINK value to use in subsequent MgO calculations.

No separate report is required. Your answers remain in the notebook.
